module gunbc.evaluation_budget_consequence_emit

import extdeps.languages.rust.string_literal { escape_rust_cooked_string_literal_body }
import std.evaluation_budget { evaluation_budget_refusal_code }
import std.types { List, NonEmptyStr, String }

// THE MACHINE IDENTITY OF A BUDGET BREACH, PROJECTED INTO THE SEED.
//
// WHY A PROJECTION AND NOT A HOST READ. `cli_run` needs the stable code at the moment it renders a
// refusal, and it had it as an independently authored literal sitting directly beneath a doc
// comment naming std.evaluation_budget as the authority -- a citation of one authority above a
// second authoring of the same fact. The alternative of reading the .dag at runtime was refused by
// the reviewing authority for reasons that are properties of the product, not preferences: the
// source available when the binary runs may differ from the source it was built from; source
// absence or a parse failure would invent runtime refusal states for a value that is compile-time
// authority; it would bypass the committed generated-artifact drift wall; the CLI would own a
// decoder and a fallback policy for a value it does not own; and the built product would no longer
// be closed over its own machine-code identity.
//
// WHY A DEDICATED ARTIFACT AND NOT A LINE IN AN EXISTING ONE. `v1_interpreter_dispatch_generated.rs`
// already reaches hand-written seed call sites, so hitchhiking there would have worked. Its SUBJECT
// is interpreter primitive dispatch; mixing a second authority into one generated file for
// convenience is the DESIGN section 3 fork wearing a generated file's clothes.
//
// THE LITERAL BELOW IS NOT AUTHORED HERE. This emitter EVALUATES evaluation_budget_refusal_code()
// and escapes the result; the string "evaluation_budget_exceeded" appears nowhere in this module.
// An emitter that spelled the value in its own template would be a third authority, and the
// authority-movement falsifier -- change the std value, regenerate, require the new value in the
// generated Rust and zero occurrences of the old one in the served response -- would pass while
// proving nothing.
//
// WHAT THE GENERATED MODULE MAY AND MAY NOT CARRY. Only the authority-derived identity. It does not
// render the JSON response body and absorbs no serving-specific formatting: that is a caller
// boundary, and moving it into a std consequence projection would mix two authorities in one
// artifact for the second time in the same construction.
fn expected_evaluation_budget_consequence_generated_rs() -> String {
  concat(
    concat(
      "// Generated by gunbc evaluation_budget_consequence_emit — do not hand-edit.\n",
      concat(
        "// Authority: std.evaluation_budget evaluation_budget_refusal_code.\n",
        "// The machine identity of a generic evaluation-budget breach. It is a projection of the\n// authority named above, not a second authoring of it: regenerate rather than edit.\n"
      )
    ),
    concat(
      "pub const EVALUATION_BUDGET_REFUSAL_CODE: &str = \"",
      concat(
        escape_rust_cooked_string_literal_body(s: evaluation_budget_refusal_code() as String),
        "\";\n"
      )
    )
  )
}

// THE FALSIFIER IS NAMED HERE AS STEPS, NOT RECORDED AS NUMBERS. DESIGN section 6 says a
// measurement is cited by naming the producer that re-derives it, so this row names the four
// instruments that answer "does the served machine identity actually come from the authority" and
// the one-token perturbation that makes them discriminate. Each step is an existing entry point;
// none of them was written for this receipt.
//
// WHY THE PERTURBATION IS PART OF THE INSTRUMENT AND NOT AN AFTERTHOUGHT: with the authority
// unchanged, every one of these steps is green whether or not the seed reads the projection at
// all, because the value it would have chosen independently is the same value. Only moving the
// authority separates "the response carries the code BECAUSE the projection carries it" from "the
// response happens to spell the same thing".
//
// STEP 3 REBUILDS BEFORE SERVING FOR THE REASON gunbc.generated_artifact_merge_driver ALREADY
// RECORDS ABOUT REGEN: a binary that predates the change it must express will report a confident
// green for the wrong reason. Learned again the expensive way on this construction, where the seed
// emitter had to be rebuilt from the installed mirror before it would place the module at all.
fn evaluation_budget_consequence_falsifier_steps() -> List<String> {
  [
    "0. perturb ONLY std.evaluation_budget evaluation_budget_refusal_code, changing nothing else",
    "1. drift arm, BEFORE regenerating: gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/instruments/generated_artifact_gate.dag --function main -- it must refuse, naming evaluation_budget_consequence_generated.rs",
    "2. regenerate with the same gate's main_wet, then read EVALUATION_BUDGET_REFUSAL_CODE out of src/v1/stage0/src/evaluation_budget_consequence_generated.rs -- it must carry the moved value",
    "3. rebuild the seed (cargo build --release --bin gunbc), then serve the breach fixture: gunbc serve --entry dag/test/fixture/serve_seam_probe.dag --function serve_budget_breach_probe --eval-budget-cpu-ms 50, and POST to it",
    "4. the 500 body must carry the MOVED code exactly once and the FORMER code exactly zero times; the refusal must come from a real thread-CPU breach, which the fixture arm produces by spinning rather than by asserting one",
    "5. revert the perturbation and regenerate, leaving the tree clean",
  ]
}
